so six years ago a nascent unborn even  cryptocurrency called zcash was about to  emerge on the scene zcash uses a new  technology a new map really called zero  knowledge proofs that lets you prove  that something is true without revealing  anything about what it is that you're  proving including not only the thing  itself but your identity this is great  for cryptocurrency because it means that  not only can you shield the values that  are being sent but you don't need to  reveal even a public key  so it's perfect true privacy ironically  in order to pull this off you have to  actually rely on human beings to do a  very elaborate process known as the  creation ceremony zcash recruited six  people from around the world each of  which participated by doing an elaborate  series  of steps that resulted in them briefly  owning  part of what's called the creation key  all six of these pieces of the key if  they were ever united would give the  owner the ability to create infinite  anonymous cryptocurrency but if only one  of these people  destroyed their part of the private  creation key that meant the ceremony had  succeeded these zero knowledge proofs  could be enacted and no one could copy  the cryptocurrency we learned the first  five identities of those people almost  immediately after the ceremony was  revealed but  the sixth person's identity was a  pseudonym john doberton we now know that  john doberton was edward snowden and he  participated uh with an agreement with  zuko wilcox the ceo of the zcash company  that created zcash to protect his  privacy until he felt safe revealing it  we  now have been able to put together a lot  of the backstory about edward snowden's  involvement what i think is most  interesting is snowden didn't just  follow orders right like everybody had  to follow a certain series of  requirements like you had to use it you  had to buy a new computer that no one  had ever used before  it had to have never been connected to  the internet so that no one online  knew anything about the computer  it was snowden himself that actually  made a crucial recommendation that ended  up being followed by every participant  basically that you have to buy this  hardware from a location that not even  you knew existed until you bought it so  like for example if you needed a  computer you had to just drive around  randomly  until you found a place that sold  computers and you had to go in and buy  that computer we were literally three  minutes before you didn't even know you  were going to be doing that this is a  tactic that azuco says the cia employs  to ensure perfect privacy and security  this is something that zcash followed at  the advice of edward snowden the last  thing i should say to be totally clear i  did not interview edward snowden for  this story i approached him and  conversed with a couple of his  representatives the video is our sole  source but i was able to interview zuko  wilcox and got some really fun little  anecdotes about the process notably  counterintuitively you know you think of  the proverbial disguise this is the  glasses with the big nose and the  mustache you know you can put on to  cover your face nodon actually did the  opposite of that because he's so known  for his glasses  that when he wanted to hide his identity  he actually took his glasses off  and apparently when um snowden walks  around moscow  if he has his glasses on um everybody  recognizes him but if he takes his  glasses off no one knows who he is  zcash is now valued at over two billion  dollars the privacy coin space is now an  11 billion dollar space according to  data from coin market cap and we're  seeing as a number of regulations go in  place that look to force uh disclosures  about personal identity and increasing  interest in this privacy coin space  you
 

